"# Appendix — Exact Ordering and Additive Inverse

Chapter 6 of Book of Math covered the core exact-rational verbs — `exact/rat`, `exact/+`, `exact/-`, `exact/*`, `exact//`, `exact/=`, `exact/<`, `exact/>`. Three companion verbs round out the interface:

## `(exact/<= a b)` — less-than-or-equal on ℚ

The standard weak ordering: `(exact/<= (exact/rat 1 2) (exact/rat 1 2))` is `#t`; `(exact/<= (exact/rat 1 3) (exact/rat 1 2))` is `#t`; `(exact/<= (exact/rat 1 2) (exact/rat 1 3))` is `#f`. Reflexive, transitive, and total on ℚ — the three properties that make `exact/<=` a real partial-order predicate rather than a partial one.

## `(exact/>= a b)` — greater-than-or-equal on ℚ

Symmetric to `exact/<=`: `(exact/>= a b) = (exact/<= b a)`. Together with `exact/<`, `exact/>`, and `exact/=`, the five comparators form a complete decision procedure for the ordering on ℚ.

Two elements `a, b ∈ ℚ` satisfy exactly one of `a < b`, `a = b`, `a > b`. The comparators are total in the sense that at least one of the five predicates returns `#t` for any pair.

## `(exact/neg a)` — additive inverse

Given `a ∈ ℚ`, `(exact/neg a)` returns the unique element `x` such that `(exact/+ a x) = 0/1`. For `(exact/rat 3 4)`, the result is `(exact/rat -3 4)`. For `(exact/rat 0 1)`, the result is itself.

`exact/neg` is an involution: `(exact/neg (exact/neg a))` equals `a` for every rational. Together with `exact/+`, it makes ℚ an abelian group under addition — the algebraic structure Chapter 6 leans on when it introduces \"opposite numbers.\"

## Composing them

```scheme
;; Sort a list of rationals ascending.
(define (sort-rationals xs)
  (if (null? xs) '()
      (let* ((pivot (car xs))
             (rest  (cdr xs))
             (less  (filter (lambda (r) (exact/< r pivot)) rest))
             (more  (filter (lambda (r) (exact/>= r pivot)) rest)))
        (append (sort-rationals less) (list pivot) (sort-rationals more)))))

;; Additive inverse commutes with sum.
(define a (exact/rat 3 4))
(define b (exact/rat 1 5))
(display (exact/= (exact/neg (exact/+ a b))
                  (exact/+ (exact/neg a) (exact/neg b))))
;; => #t
```

See Book of Math Chapter 6 for the introduction of exact rationals; see also Chapter 15 (Multivariable + Linear Algebra) for how the ordering extends to vectors and matrices.
"
